  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "I suggest to include overall file size of avatar (.vrca file) into counts.\nThat's because some users adding rly heavy content into avatars: very long music, unnecesary huge uncompressed textures (especially when there is also too many unatlased materials). That causes noticeble longer in-game avatar downloading and preparing. This metric may be not very useful comparing to other metrics, becuase other users can see it when avatar is loaded and it no more makes sense, but it can be used to inform avatar creators that media content in avatar is too big and recommend to reduce it's size.\n\nThis also can motivate creators to use crunch compression: textures are one of heaviest parts of avatar build. At 100% texture compressor quality it's almost no noticeble differences in quality, hovever it reduses texture asset size ~5 times and in some specific cases (like flat-colored painting with smooth bounds) up to ~10 times. I believe, if everyone used crunch compression, overall avatar loading time would noticeably increase and traffic for content servers decrease.",
